---
import Layout from "@layouts/Layout.astro";
import { resources, siteUrl } from "@features/discovery/catalog";
import { rateLimit } from "@features/discovery/rate-limits";
---

<Layout>
  <main
    class="mx-auto min-h-screen max-w-3xl px-4 py-16 text-foreground md:px-8"
  >
    <h1 class="mb-6 animate-fade-up text-4xl font-semibold">Developers</h1>
    <div class="flex animate-fade-up animation-delay-100 flex-col gap-8 text-lg leading-relaxed">
      <p>
        m4t.tf is machine-readable. Every page is available as HTML or markdown,
        and the files below expose the site's content and metadata as data.
        Everything is public: there are no API keys, and requests are not
        metered.
      </p>

      <section class="flex flex-col gap-3">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">Machine-readable files</h2>
        <p>
          Each entry is an unauthenticated GET. The human-readable pages are
          served by the same interface.
        </p>
        <ul class="flex flex-col gap-3">
          {
            resources.map((resource) => (
              <li>
                <a
                  href={resource.path}
                  class="underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-foreground/70"
                >
                  {resource.path}
                </a>
                <span class="block text-base text-muted-foreground">
                  {resource.type} — {resource.description}
                </span>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>
        <p>
          These paths are intentionally absent rather than missing: there is no
          file at <code>/.well-known/openid-configuration</code>,
          <code>/.well-known/oauth-authorization-server</code>, or
          <code>/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource</code>, because the site
          has no authentication and no protected APIs.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="flex flex-col gap-3">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">Quickstart</h2>
        <p>
          Send <code>Accept: text/markdown</code> to any page to receive it as markdown
          instead of HTML. Appending <code>.md</code> does the same for a page, and
          returns a markdown summary for a machine-readable file.
        </p>
        <p>
          Send <code>Accept: application/json</code> to a machine-readable file to
          receive it as JSON: JSON resources return their canonical document, and the
          others return a typed descriptor with the resource's metadata.
        </p>
        <pre
          class="overflow-x-auto rounded border border-border bg-muted p-4 text-base"><code>{`curl -H "Accept: text/markdown" ${siteUrl}/cv
curl ${siteUrl}/about.md
curl -H "Accept: application/json" ${siteUrl}/llms.txt`}</code></pre>
        <p>
          Nonexistent paths return a real HTTP 404, so a 404 is proof the path
          does not exist. Responses carry <code
            >Vary: Accept, Accept-Encoding</code
          >, so caches must store the HTML and markdown representations
          separately.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="flex flex-col gap-3">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">Rate limits</h2>
        <p>
          Requests are not metered. Every response carries
          <code>RateLimit-Policy</code>, <code>RateLimit-Limit</code>, and
          <code>RateLimit-Reset</code> declaring a published floor of
          <code>{rateLimit.quota}</code> requests per minute per client. The
          origin will not reject you below that floor, so the site never returns
          <code>429 Too Many Requests</code> for rate limiting.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="flex flex-col gap-3">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">Errors</h2>
        <p>
          A failed request returns a representation matching your
          <code>Accept</code> header. Send <code>Accept: application/json</code>
          to receive an RFC 9457 <code>application/problem+json</code> document,
          or <code>Accept: text/markdown</code> to receive the same problem as
          markdown. A request to a machine-readable file or a path under
          <code>/.well-known/</code> also receives JSON, and a browser
          navigation still receives the HTML error page. Every error carries a
          stable <code>code</code> and a recovery <code>resolution</code>
          alongside the human-readable <code>detail</code> and the
          <code>documentation_url</code> for this page.
        </p>
        <pre
          class="overflow-x-auto rounded border border-border bg-muted p-4 text-base"><code>{`curl -H "Accept: application/json" ${siteUrl}/no-such-page
curl -H "Accept: text/markdown" ${siteUrl}/no-such-page`}</code></pre>
        <pre
          class="overflow-x-auto rounded border border-border bg-muted p-4 text-base"><code>{`{
  "type": "${siteUrl}/problems/not-found",
  "title": "Resource not found",
  "status": 404,
  "detail": "No resource exists at the requested path. The published resources are listed at ${siteUrl}/llms.txt.",
  "code": "RESOURCE_NOT_FOUND",
  "resolution": "Fetch ${siteUrl}/llms.txt to discover the published resources, or read ${siteUrl}/developers for the interface.",
  "documentation_url": "${siteUrl}/developers",
  "instance": "/no-such-page"
}`}</code></pre>
        <pre
          class="overflow-x-auto rounded border border-border bg-muted p-4 text-base"><code>{`# Resource not found

No resource exists at the requested path. The published resources are listed at ${siteUrl}/llms.txt.

Resolution: Fetch ${siteUrl}/llms.txt to discover the published resources, or read ${siteUrl}/developers for the interface.

- Documentation: ${siteUrl}/developers
- Site guide: ${siteUrl}/llms.txt
- Sitemap: ${siteUrl}/sitemap.xml`}</code></pre>
        <p>
          A non-GET request to a machine-readable file returns
          <code>405 Method Not Allowed</code> with an <code>Allow</code> header,
          and a request whose <code>Accept</code> header excludes every
          supported representation returns <code>406 Not Acceptable</code>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="versioning-and-deprecation" class="flex flex-col gap-3">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">Versioning and deprecation</h2>
        <p>
          Send the <code>API-Version</code> header to declare the API
          compatibility version you expect. The current version is
          <code>1</code>, which is also the default when the header is absent.
          Non-breaking changes stay within a version; a breaking change takes a
          new value, while the previous version is deprecated rather than
          changed in place.
        </p>
        <p>
          Deprecated resources return RFC 9745
          <code>Deprecation</code> and RFC 8594 <code>Sunset</code> response
          headers, plus a <code>Link</code> header with
          <code>rel="deprecation"</code> and
          <code>rel="successor-version"</code>. A deprecated resource keeps
          working for at least six months after its deprecation date, until the
          <code>Sunset</code> date, after which it returns
          <code>410 Gone</code>. The OpenAPI document declares both headers.
        </p>
      </section>

      <p>
        The whole interface is described by the OpenAPI 3.1 document at
        <a
          href="/openapi.json"
          class="underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-foreground/70"
          >/openapi.json</a
        >, and
        <a
          href="/llms.txt"
          class="underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-foreground/70"
          >/llms.txt</a
        > indexes everything the site publishes.
      </p>
    </div>
  </main>
</Layout>
